Check 1. Expect: "packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js <file> prints <file>:<line>: <error> for every row that is not valid JSON (1-based lines, blank lines skipped, an unterminated last line checked) and exits 1 if any, 0 if none"
Run:
`d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{"a":1}\n\n{bad\n[1,2]\n\n{"b":2' > "$d/mixed.jsonl"; node packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js "$d/mixed.jsonl"; echo "exit=$?"`
`printf '{"a":1}\n{"b":' > "$d/torn.jsonl"; node packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js "$d/torn.jsonl"; echo "exit=$?"`
`printf '{"a":1}\n\n[2]\n"s"\n' > "$d/clean.jsonl"; node packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js "$d/clean.jsonl"; echo "exit=$?"`
`printf '{"a":1}\n{"b":2}' > "$d/clean2.jsonl"; node packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js "$d/clean2.jsonl"; echo "exit=$?"`
PASS if all of these hold. mixed.jsonl prints exactly two stdout lines, one starting with `<path>:3: ` and one with `<path>:6: `, where `<path>` is the path exactly as passed, and each line has non-empty error text after the prefix; it reports no other line (in particular not the blank lines 2 and 5) and exits 1. torn.jsonl prints exactly one line starting with `<path>:2: ` followed by non-empty error text, and exits 1. clean.jsonl and clean2.jsonl print nothing to stdout and exit 0. FAIL if any of these differ: a missing or extra reported line, a wrong line number (0-based, or blank lines not counted), a blank line reported, the unterminated last row not reported, a different prefix format, or a wrong exit code.

Check 2. Expect: "A missing file or no argument prints to stderr and exits 2"
Run:
`node packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js > /tmp/jl-out1 2> /tmp/jl-err1; echo "exit=$?"; wc -c < /tmp/jl-err1`
`node packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js /nonexistent/nope.jsonl > /tmp/jl-out2 2> /tmp/jl-err2; echo "exit=$?"; wc -c < /tmp/jl-err2`
PASS if both commands print `exit=2` and both stderr files are non-empty (the byte count is greater than 0). FAIL if either exit code is not 2 or either stderr file is empty.

Check 3. Expect: "packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js exports lint(text) returning [{ line, error }]"
Run:
`node --input-type=module -e "import { lint } from './packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js'; const r = lint('{\"a\":1}\n\n{bad\n[1]\n{\"b\":'); console.log(JSON.stringify(r)); console.log(JSON.stringify(lint('{\"a\":1}\n\n[2]\n')));"; echo "exit=$?"`
PASS if the import works, the first printed value is an array of exactly two objects, each with a numeric `line` and a non-empty string `error`, the lines are 3 and 5, the second printed value is `[]`, and the command prints `exit=0`. FAIL if `lint` is not a named export, the import fails, the return value is not an array of `{ line, error }` objects, or the line numbers or the empty result differ.

